reims-vgpu: half-float frames convert with the CPU's half-float instructions.

The present capture and the render Store's stash convert the compositor's
RGBA16F front surface (1206x2622 on the vphone) into BGRA8 through a
65 536-entry table, one lookup per channel, and then walk the result again for
present_content's rgb_nz and max_rgb. Where the CPU has F16C and AVX2, the
whole pixels now go through vcvtph2ps four at a time, with the table's own
rule (NaN or f <= 0 gives 0, f >= 1 gives 255, else (f * 255 + 0.5) as u8, in
f32 with a separate multiply and add), and the figures are taken in the same
pass; the table converts the tail and every other plan. A test holds the
vector arm to the table, bytes and figures, for every half-float word in
every channel slot, and the figures to bgra_rgb_stats on black, alpha-only and
mixed frames.

convert_band_streamed writes the vector arm's pixels with non-temporal stores
(runtime::stream_copy, also added here: an SSE2 streaming copy and its
fence), for a writer whose output the CPU does not read back at once.

Offline on the development host (i9-13900K, shared with other builds;
conversion_timing, medians of 31, two runs): one thread converting the frame
with its figures, table 4.1 ms against vector 1.9 ms, the figures walk alone
0.7 ms; the present capture's four-band conversion with caches evicted first,
1.9-2.0 ms against 1.7-1.8 ms. Not measured on a live phone.

